        "gg2oLi7pwjo_text": "Veterinary Health News",
        "gg4-9RDfcI-_text": "<p><span style=\"background-color:transparent;color:#000000;font-size:17px;\">Dr. Martinez explained why none of my attempts worked:</span></p><p>&nbsp;</p><p><span style=\"background-color:transparent;color:#000000;font-size:17px;\"><strong>Exercise:</strong> <i>\"Over-tired dogs actually have MORE sleep problems. Their stress hormones spike, keeping them wired despite physical exhaustion.</i>\"</span></p><p>&nbsp;</p><p><span style=\"background-color:transparent;color:#000000;font-size:17px;\"><strong>Training:</strong> \"<i>You can't train a dog out of broken brain chemistry.\"</i></span></p><p>&nbsp;</p><p><span style=\"background-color:transparent;color:#000000;font-size:17px;\"><strong>Calming products:</strong> \"<i>They target temporary anxiety, not the sleep system itself. They might work for an hour, then wear off.\"</i></span></p><p>&nbsp;</p><p><span style=\"background-color:transparent;color:#000000;font-size:17px;\"><strong>Prescription meds:</strong> \"<i>Sedatives force unconsciousness, but don't restore natural sleep cycles.\"</i></span></p>",
